<%
/* =========================================================================
   ONE FORM FIELD — the shared field component for every dashboard form.

   WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Every admin form was written by hand, one page at a
   time, so each page invented its own spelling of the same field. Before this
   partial: `fullName` was half-width on addStudent and full-width on
   editStudent; the same three time inputs carried `input--ltr dir="ltr"` on
   the add form and none of it on the edit form; the empty option was worded
   five different ways ("— اختر —", "اختر المادة…", "— بدون —",
   "— غير محددة —", "— غير محدد —"); two conflicting sets of grade labels
   rendered one page apart; and one page emitted `aria-describedby=""`, which
   references an element that cannot exist. None of that was a decision. It is
   what happens when the same markup is retyped eleven times.

   USAGE — one `fld` object, passed as the include's data:

     include("_field", { fld: { type: "select", name: "grade", id: "grade",
                                label: "الصف", list: "grades",
                                value: pre.grade, required: true } })

   (Written with EJS delimiters at the call site. They are left out of this
   comment on purpose: EJS tokenises the file by delimiter, so a literal
   opening tag inside a scriptlet comment is read as a real tag and the compile
   fails with "Could not find matching close tag".)

   WHY THE OPTIONS ARE NESTED IN ONE OBJECT rather than passed flat. EJS builds
   an include's scope from the PARENT's locals with the include data layered on
   top, so a flat option this partial reads but the call site did not pass
   resolves to the parent's local of that name. That is not hypothetical:
   lesson.ejs has `var rows` for its table, and a flat `rows` option would make
   every field on that page inherit it. Nesting means exactly one name — `fld`
   — can collide, and nothing in the app is called that.

   WHAT IT OWNS, i.e. the things that kept drifting:
     · the markup shape — .form-field › .label › control › .field-hint
     · ONE neutral-option wording, "اختر <الحقل>…", built from the label
     · ONE set of option presets, so the grade labels cannot fork again
     · `input--ltr dir="ltr" inputmode` on every type whose value is a Latin
       run — the exact drift described above
     · `aria-describedby` pointing at the error, else the hint, else OMITTED

   WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN. Nothing here changes what the server receives: `name`,
   `id`, `value`, `required`, `pattern`, `accept`, `maxlength` and every raw
   attribute pass straight through. _question-block.ejs and _table-toolbar.ejs
   deliberately do NOT route through this partial — the first is bound to
   admin.js by exact class names and its `__I__` index placeholder, the second
   has a working local helper — so both keep their markup and had only their
   classes corrected.

   fld OPTIONS
     type      text|tel|url|number|password|datetime-local|time|file|
               textarea|select                                  (default text)
     name      submitted key. id defaults to it.
     label     Arabic label text. Also builds the neutral option.
     value     current value. For select, matched against each option's value.
     required  adds `required` AND the <abbr> star.
     hint      explanatory line under the control.
     hintHtml  a hint containing markup, emitted unescaped. Exists for one
               reason: a hint that quotes a Latin run needs
               `<span dir="ltr">01:30</span>` around it or the bidi algorithm
               reorders it mid-sentence. AUTHOR-WRITTEN LITERALS ONLY — never a
               value from the request or the database. Use `hint` for anything
               that is not markup you typed yourself.
     error     string, or an Ajv-style { message }. Adds --error and
               aria-invalid, and replaces the hint.
     width     sm|md|lg — caps the CONTROL (see "field widths" in admin.css).
     full      spans both columns of a .form-grid.
     list      "levels"|"grades"|"groups", an array of { value, label }, or an
               array of { label, items:[…] } to emit <optgroup>s. An option may
               also carry `disabled: true`.
     neutral   override the empty-option text; false to omit it entirely.
     unknown   false to suppress the "stored value not in the list" option.
     ltr       force the Latin-run treatment on or off.
     tall      textarea only — the taller variant, for a model answer.
     rows      textarea only                                       (default 3)
     attrs     raw extra attributes, e.g. ' data-qtype'.
     cls       extra classes on the .form-field wrapper.
     Passed through when present: placeholder, maxlength, min, max, step,
     pattern, accept, autocomplete, inputmode.

   `fld` itself is read behind a `typeof` guard: EJS compiles with
   `with(locals)`, so naming a key the caller did not pass is a ReferenceError
   — and a render error bypasses the route's try/catch, because Express hands
   it to its own error handler.
   ====================================================================== */

var F = typeof fld !== "undefined" && fld && typeof fld === "object" ? fld : {};

var fType = F.type ? String(F.type) : "text";
var fName = F.name ? String(F.name) : "";
var fId = F.id ? String(F.id) : fName;
var fLabel = F.label ? String(F.label) : "";
var fReq = F.required ? true : false;
var fHint = F.hint ? String(F.hint) : "";
/* Kept separate from fHint all the way to the two emit branches at the bottom,
   so there is exactly one place in this file that writes unescaped text and it
   is visible at the point of use. */
var fHintHtml = F.hintHtml ? String(F.hintHtml) : "";
var fWidth = F.width ? String(F.width) : "";
var fTall = F.tall ? true : false;
var fAttrs = F.attrs ? String(F.attrs) : "";
var fRows = F.rows ? String(F.rows) : "3";

/* An Ajv error object, a plain string, or nothing. */
var fErr = "";
if (F.error) fErr = typeof F.error === "string" ? F.error : String(F.error.message || "");

var fValue = F.value === undefined || F.value === null ? "" : String(F.value);

/* ------------------------------------------------------ option presets ----
   The DB stores English values and the page shows Arabic labels, so these
   pairs are a contract with the schema and must not be edited casually. The
   grade labels are the long form — "الصف الأول", not "الأول". Both spellings
   were in use, on pages that render one after the other. */
var PRESETS = {
  levels: [
    { value: "biology", label: "الأحياء" },
    { value: "geology", label: "الجيولوجيا" }
  ],
  grades: [
    { value: "1st", label: "الصف الأول" },
    { value: "2nd", label: "الصف الثاني" },
    { value: "3rd", label: "الصف الثالث" }
  ],
  groups: [
    { value: "OnlineStudent", label: "أونلاين" },
    { value: "Saturday", label: "السبت" },
    { value: "Sunday", label: "الأحد" },
    { value: "Monday", label: "الاثنين" },
    { value: "Tuesday", label: "الثلاثاء" },
    { value: "Wednesday", label: "الأربعاء" },
    { value: "Thursday", label: "الخميس" },
    { value: "Friday", label: "الجمعة" }
  ]
};

var fList = [];
if (typeof F.list === "string") fList = PRESETS[F.list] || [];
else if (Array.isArray(F.list)) fList = F.list;

/* ------------------------------------------------- the unknown-value option --
   A stored value that is not in the list has to be shown, or the select renders
   with nothing matching, the browser silently selects the first option, and the
   next save writes THAT over the real value. The admin never sees it happen.

   editStudent.ejs had this escape hatch hand-written for `educetionlevel` and
   `group` and not for `grade` — the reason being that the level list is the one
   that demonstrably forked (three other admin views offer Secondary/Preparatory
   for the same field, values the student-facing queries can never match), so it
   got the guard and the field next to it did not. The exposure is identical for
   every select on the dashboard, so it is handled here for all of them, once.

   The label says why the value looks odd rather than just echoing it: an admin
   who reads "Secondary (قيمة غير معروفة)" knows to pick a real value; one who
   reads "Secondary" assumes it is a normal choice.

   `unknown: false` opts out, for a select whose value legitimately lives
   outside its option list. */
var fSelected = false;
if (fType === "select" && fValue !== "" && F.unknown !== false) {
  for (var ki = 0; ki < fList.length; ki++) {
    var kop = fList[ki];
    if (!kop) continue;
    if (Array.isArray(kop.items)) {
      for (var kj = 0; kj < kop.items.length; kj++) {
        if (kop.items[kj] && String(kop.items[kj].value) === fValue) fSelected = true;
      }
    } else if (String(kop.value) === fValue) {
      fSelected = true;
    }
  }
  if (!fSelected) {
    /* A copy — fList may be one of the shared PRESETS arrays, and pushing onto
       that would leak this record's stray value into every later field on the
       page. The presets are module-level state for the duration of a render. */
    fList = fList.concat([{ value: fValue, label: fValue + " (قيمة غير معروفة)" }]);
  }
}

/* ONE neutral wording, built from the label: "المادة" gives "اختر المادة…".
   `neutral: false` drops the empty option — for a select that always holds a
   value, like the student/admin role. */
var fNeutral = fLabel ? "اختر " + fLabel + "…" : "اختر…";
var fHasNeutral = true;
if (F.neutral === false) fHasNeutral = false;
else if (F.neutral) fNeutral = String(F.neutral);

/* --------------------------------------------------------- Latin runs -----
   A URL, a phone number, a mark, a datetime-local value: all Latin digit or
   ASCII runs, which the bidi algorithm reorders inside an RTL line — a URL
   renders with its slashes in the wrong places and "00:30" can read "30:00".
   Centralising it here is the whole point: this is precisely what one form
   remembered and the next forgot. */
var LTR_TYPES = {
  url: "url", tel: "tel", number: "numeric", email: "email",
  password: "", "datetime-local": "", time: ""
};
var fLtr = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(LTR_TYPES, fType);
if (F.ltr !== undefined) fLtr = F.ltr ? true : false;

var fInputmode = F.inputmode ? String(F.inputmode) : fLtr ? LTR_TYPES[fType] || "" : "";

/* --------------------------------------------------- assembled classes ----
   A <select> wears .select and never .input: the two are sized identically,
   but only .select carries the arrow inset and cursor: pointer, so a
   <select class="input"> is a dropdown that does not look clickable. */
var ctlClass = fType === "select" ? "select" : "input";
if (fLtr) ctlClass += " input--ltr";
if (fType === "textarea" && fTall) ctlClass += " input--tall";

var wrapClass = "form-field";
if (fWidth === "sm" || fWidth === "md" || fWidth === "lg") wrapClass += " form-field--" + fWidth;
if (F.full) wrapClass += " form-grid__full";
if (fErr) wrapClass += " form-field--error";
if (F.cls) wrapClass += " " + String(F.cls);

/* aria-describedby names the error when there is one, else the hint, and is
   OMITTED when there is neither. Conditional on fId as well: the description
   is referenced BY id, so with no id there is nothing to point at and the
   attribute would name an element that cannot exist — the same invalid
   reference this partial exists to remove, reintroduced from the other end. */
var describedBy = !fId ? "" : fErr ? fId + "-error" : fHint || fHintHtml ? fId + "-hint" : "";

/* Only the attributes the caller actually passed. `min: 0` and `maxlength: 0`
   are real values, so the test is against undefined and empty string rather
   than falsiness — the obvious `||` here silently drops every min="0". */
var PASS = ["placeholder", "maxlength", "min", "max", "step", "pattern", "accept", "autocomplete"];
var passed = [];
for (var _p = 0; _p < PASS.length; _p++) {
  var _v = F[PASS[_p]];
  if (_v === undefined || _v === null || _v === "") continue;
  passed.push(
    " " + PASS[_p] + '="' +
    String(_v).replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/"/g, "&quot;").replace(/</g, "&lt;") +
    '"'
  );
}
var passStr = passed.join("");

/* Built as strings so the tag below stays on one line and renders as one line.
   The trim-newline closing delimiter is used throughout for the same reason:
   without newline trimming a field emits its markup buried in twenty blank
   lines, and View Source is where the next person debugs a form. (That
   delimiter is described rather than typed here for the reason given at the top
   of this file — EJS tokenises the whole file by delimiter before it parses
   anything, so a literal one inside a comment ends the scriptlet mid-sentence
   and the rest of the comment compiles as template text.) */
var idAttr = fId ? ' id="' + fId + '"' : "";
var forAttr = fId ? ' for="' + fId + '"' : "";
var reqAttr = fReq ? " required" : "";
var descAttr = describedBy ? ' aria-describedby="' + describedBy + '"' : "";
var invAttr = fErr ? ' aria-invalid="true"' : "";
-%>
<div class="<%= wrapClass %>">
  <label class="label"<%- forAttr %>><%= fLabel %><% if (fReq) { %> <abbr title="مطلوب">*</abbr><% } %></label>
<% if (fType === "select") { -%>
  <select class="<%= ctlClass %>"<%- idAttr %> name="<%= fName %>"<%- reqAttr %><%- descAttr %><%- invAttr %><%- fAttrs %>>
<% if (fHasNeutral) { -%>
    <option value=""><%= fNeutral %></option>
<% } -%>
<% for (var oi = 0; oi < fList.length; oi++) { var op = fList[oi]; -%>
<% if (op && Array.isArray(op.items)) { -%>
    <optgroup label="<%= op.label %>">
<% for (var oj = 0; oj < op.items.length; oj++) { var sub = op.items[oj]; -%>
      <option value="<%= sub.value %>"<%- sub.disabled ? " disabled" : "" %><%- String(sub.value) === fValue ? " selected" : "" %>><%= sub.label %></option>
<% } -%>
    </optgroup>
<% } else if (op) { -%>
    <option value="<%= op.value %>"<%- op.disabled ? " disabled" : "" %><%- String(op.value) === fValue ? " selected" : "" %>><%= op.label %></option>
<% } -%>
<% } -%>
  </select>
<% } else if (fType === "textarea") { -%>
<%
  /* rows= stays on the element as the no-CSS floor; textarea.input sets the
     real min-block-size and resize: vertical. A bare <textarea> gets the
     browser default resize: both, which lets it be dragged wider than its
     RTL container. */
-%>
  <textarea class="<%= ctlClass %>"<%- idAttr %> name="<%= fName %>" rows="<%= fRows %>"<%- reqAttr %><%- descAttr %><%- invAttr %><%- passStr %><%- fAttrs %>><%= fValue %></textarea>
<% } else { -%>
<%
  /* A password is never echoed back into an input, even on a failed
     submission — the value would sit in the HTML, in the browser's cache and
     in any proxy log between here and the admin. Retyping it is the point. A
     file input has no settable value at all: assigning one is a security error
     in every browser, since a page could otherwise post a file of its
     choosing from the admin's disk. */
-%>
  <input class="<%= ctlClass %>" type="<%= fType %>"<%- idAttr %> name="<%= fName %>"<% if (fType !== "password" && fType !== "file") { %> value="<%= fValue %>"<% } %><%- fLtr ? ' dir="ltr"' : "" %><%- fInputmode ? ' inputmode="' + fInputmode + '"' : "" %><%- reqAttr %><%- descAttr %><%- invAttr %><%- passStr %><%- fAttrs %> />
<% } -%>
<%
  /* The error REPLACES the hint rather than stacking under it: two lines of
     small type, one grey and one red, under a single control is how a form
     starts reading as a wall of warnings. The error is the more urgent of the
     two and the hint returns as soon as the field is fixed. */
-%>
<% if (fErr) { -%>
  <p class="field-hint field-hint--error"<%- fId ? ' id="' + fId + '-error"' : "" %>><%= fErr %></p>
<% } else if (fHint) { -%>
  <p class="field-hint"<%- fId ? ' id="' + fId + '-hint"' : "" %>><%= fHint %></p>
<% } else if (fHintHtml) { -%>
  <p class="field-hint"<%- fId ? ' id="' + fId + '-hint"' : "" %>><%- fHintHtml %></p>
<% } -%>
</div>
